#!/bin/bash
# 732 (Sat 28 Nov 2026 14:20, Peter: "This needs to be fixed before I ask you. You need to create a bot that asks the questions
# before I do and fixes the issues, it's not acceptable"). Three recorders wrote nothing for 32 hours and the thin markets were
# not on the fast feed; the watchman wrote NOT FIXED and nothing acted. Builds the bot that asks and fixes. Starts after the
# 15:00 kick-offs so nothing is restarted around them. Places nothing. Nothing deleted.
[ "$(date -u +%s)" -ge "$(date -u -d '2026-11-28 15:06:00' +%s)" ] || exit 75
B=/root/pe-builder
cat > $B/BRIEF_asks_first.md <<'EOF'
You are a Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server. Read /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md first. Never an order call; do not touch /root/REAL_MONEY_OFF, /root/pe-real/placer.py, or the dry runner while its 2-day test runs (until 30 Nov 07:00 UTC) except to watch it; nothing deleted; archive copy before any edit; no secrets written anywhere; no emails, no push messages, no Claude.ai tasks - everything lives on this server. Keep memory under 2 GB. Until 17:15 UK today do not restart any recorder that is writing.
WHY: on 28 Nov at 14:04 Peter asked "are we all set for this afternoon's football" and the answer was no: three programs had written nothing for 32 hours after a database restart (processes alive, so the mechanic saw nothing; the watchman wrote NOT FIXED each run and nothing acted on it); the fast price feed covered only the 40 most-traded of 91 matches before kick-off, which leaves out exactly the thin markets our one winning trial rule (T1) trades; a waiting job (728) misfired on a text note and nobody noticed. Peter: "This needs to be fixed before I ask you. You need to create a bot that asks the questions before I do and fixes the issues. It's not acceptable." Read /root/pe-queue/reports/pre3pm.md and the 28 Nov section of safe_path_727.md.
BUILD THE BOT THAT ASKS FIRST (/root/pe-asker, run from the mechanic every 5 minutes for the cheap checks and as its own loop for the rest; one lock, so it never runs twice):
1. IT ASKS WHAT PETER WOULD ASK, of the OUTPUT not the process: for every recorder, collector, feed, loop and report on this server (build the full list by reading cron, watch.list, running processes and the tables they write - not from memory - and keep it in a table asker.things with: what it is in plain words, the table or file it writes, how fresh it must be when events are on and when nothing is on, how to restart it, and who depends on it), is the last row or file fresh enough? Is its log repeating the same error? Did any queue job end with an error message in its output even with exit 0, or sit waiting longer than its date? Is the database, disk, memory, the clock, each exchange login fine? A new program that writes to the database and is not on the list is itself a finding.
2. IT LOOKS AHEAD: every 10 minutes, from the exchange's own schedule, the next 3 hours - how many events start, in which sports, and will every one of them be recorded on every feed we use (the fast stream, the order book, the extra market types, the other two exchanges, live facts)? Fix the cover now: the fast feed's pre-kick-off limit of 40 and the cap of 200 must stop being limits - use more stream connections or whatever Betfair's documented limits allow for our key (read the documentation the recorder cites; no purchases), prioritise so that markets a trial or paper rule will act on (T1's thin football markets first) are never the ones dropped, and prove on the next busy block that every scheduled match had a price in the 30 seconds before kick-off. Do the widening tonight after 17:15 UK or tomorrow before 10:00 UK, not around a kick-off block, with the old recorder kept ready to put back.
3. IT FIXES: for each failing thing, in order - the known safe action for it (reconnect, restart that one process, re-run that one job), then check the output is fresh again; if it is not, put back what it changed and start a repair session (the same way sessions are started here: the builder command with a brief written by the asker naming the thing, the evidence, the limits above, 60 minutes, and the instruction to prove the fix by output). At most one repair session at a time, at most two attempts per thing per day, and never: an order call, the money switch, the placer, deleting data, changing a trial or paper rule, spending money. Give every database program on the server the reconnect fix (list them; originals archived), so the next automatic update cannot repeat 27 Nov.
4. IT WRITES ONE PAGE, /root/pe-queue/reports/asked_first.md, rewritten every 10 minutes and pushed only when something changed: line 1 "ALL RECORDING, nothing needs you" or "BROKEN NOW: ..." ; then FIXED WITHOUT YOU in the last 24 h (what, when it broke, when it was noticed, when fixed, what was lost); then COULD NOT FIX (what, what was tried, what it needs); then NEXT 3 HOURS (events starting, all covered yes/no). Plain English, phone length. The watchman's NOT FIXED lines and the breaks.md lines must also feed step 3, so nothing is only reported.
5. PROVE IT before you finish, by breaking things on purpose where it is safe (not around a kick-off block, not the dry runner): close a recorder's database session and show the asker noticed within its interval and it recovered; stop a side collector and show it restarted; plant a job result with an error line and show it was caught; show the three programs from today (coherence_paper, v3_recorder, desk) are on the list and fresh. Add the asker itself to the mechanic so that if the asker stops, it is restarted, and the mechanic's own heartbeat is checked by the asker.
REPORT: asked_first.md as above, plus /root/pe-queue/reports/asker_732.md, one screen: what it watches (count), what it can fix alone, the proof from step 5 with times, what it found broken on its first full pass, and what it cannot do. Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/asked_first.md reports/asker_732.md && git commit -qm "the bot that asks first: watches output, looks 3 hours ahead, fixes, one page" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Push a first asked_first.md within 90 minutes. Then end the session; the asker keeps running.
EOF
cat > $B/run_asks_first.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M); mkdir -p /root/pe-asker
cd /root/pe-asker && timeout -k 60 28800 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_asks_first.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 700 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_asks_first.log 2>&1
echo "asks-first session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x $B/run_asks_first.sh
nohup setsid $B/run_asks_first.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null &
